1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,697
Welcome to Code Quest, Chapter 5.

2
00:00:02,717 --> 00:00:04,940
This is your first boss level!

3
00:00:04,967 --> 00:00:06,261
No new topic today.

4
00:00:06,281 --> 00:00:10,687
You will build a real tool with everything
from Chapters 1 to 4.

5
00:00:10,733 --> 00:00:15,251
And an AI assistant will make two
confident mistakes about money.

6
00:00:15,271 --> 00:00:17,135
Your tests will catch both.

7
00:00:17,753 --> 00:00:21,592
Four friends finish lunch at the Corner
Cafe.

8
00:00:21,612 --> 00:00:23,135
The bill is 37.45.

9
00:00:23,155 --> 00:00:28,193
With a 15 percent tip, the receipt says
43.07, to the cent.

10
00:00:28,233 --> 00:00:32,346
One friend divides 43.07 by 4 on a phone
calculator.

11
00:00:32,366 --> 00:00:34,333
Each person pays 10.7675.

12
00:00:34,367 --> 00:00:36,288
Nobody can pay that.

13
00:00:36,308 --> 00:00:40,657
So they round it, and each friend pays
10.77.

14
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:44,200
So how much do they pay together?

15
00:00:55,580 --> 00:00:56,032
43.08.

16
00:00:56,052 --> 00:00:59,883
That is one cent more than the real total,
43.07.

17
00:00:59,903 --> 00:01:03,970
Somebody paid a cent too much, and nobody
knows who.

18
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:04,537
Small?

19
00:01:04,557 --> 00:01:04,908
Yes.

20
00:01:04,928 --> 00:01:08,343
But a cafe serves hundreds of tables.

21
00:01:08,363 --> 00:01:12,799
Asha wants a tool that gets it right every
time.

22
00:01:13,383 --> 00:01:14,733
Here is the mission.

23
00:01:14,753 --> 00:01:20,004
Asha's tool asks three questions: the
bill, the tip percent and the number of

24
00:01:20,024 --> 00:01:20,483
people.

25
00:01:20,533 --> 00:01:24,413
It prints a neat receipt, like the one
from Chapter 3.

26
00:01:24,433 --> 00:01:27,513
It tells each person exactly what to pay.

27
00:01:27,533 --> 00:01:32,126
And if the money does not share out
evenly, it says how many cents are left

28
00:01:32,146 --> 00:01:32,433
over.

29
00:01:33,053 --> 00:01:34,913
And you already know every piece.

30
00:01:34,933 --> 00:01:38,423
From Chapter 1, print, and the top to
bottom order.

31
00:01:38,467 --> 00:01:43,625
From Chapter 2, variables, division with
one slash and two slashes, and the

32
00:01:43,645 --> 00:01:44,937
remainder operator.

33
00:01:44,967 --> 00:01:48,227
From Chapter 3, f strings for neat
columns.

34
00:01:48,267 --> 00:01:52,735
And from Chapter 4, questions with input,
and converting the answers.

35
00:01:53,317 --> 00:01:54,841
Here is where we are going.

36
00:01:54,861 --> 00:01:56,957
You do not need to read the code yet.

37
00:01:56,977 --> 00:01:58,787
Look at the output on the right.

38
00:01:58,833 --> 00:02:03,170
Three friends share a bill of 48, with a
10 percent tip.

39
00:02:03,190 --> 00:02:05,503
The tool asks three questions.

40
00:02:05,533 --> 00:02:09,435
Then it prints the bill, the tip and the
total, lined up in one column, 24

41
00:02:09,455 --> 00:02:10,313
characters wide.

42
00:02:10,333 --> 00:02:15,533
Each person pays 17.60, and there are no
extra cents this time.

43
00:02:15,567 --> 00:02:17,187
About twenty lines of code.

44
00:02:17,207 --> 00:02:20,590
By the end of this video, you will
understand every one.

45
00:02:21,183 --> 00:02:24,379
Before building, Asha writes down what
done means.

46
00:02:24,399 --> 00:02:26,758
These are called acceptance criteria.

47
00:02:26,778 --> 00:02:29,523
The tool is finished when all six are
true.

48
00:02:29,567 --> 00:02:29,902
One.

49
00:02:29,922 --> 00:02:33,717
It asks the three questions, in this
order.

50
00:02:33,767 --> 00:02:34,040
Two.

51
00:02:34,060 --> 00:02:36,900
The bill can have decimals, like 37.45.

52
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:41,227
The tip percent and the number of people
are whole numbers.

53
00:02:41,267 --> 00:02:41,716
Three.

54
00:02:41,736 --> 00:02:44,927
Money shows two decimal places, lined up.

55
00:02:44,967 --> 00:02:45,338
Four.

56
00:02:45,358 --> 00:02:48,707
Each share is rounded down to a whole
cent.

57
00:02:48,733 --> 00:02:49,064
Five.

58
00:02:49,084 --> 00:02:54,682
The extra cents are shown, so the shares
and the extra cents add up to the total

59
00:02:54,702 --> 00:02:55,243
exactly.

60
00:02:55,267 --> 00:02:55,784
And six.

61
00:02:55,804 --> 00:02:59,682
It passes at least four tests that you
worked out by hand.

62
00:03:00,287 --> 00:03:02,354
Quick check, using criterion two.

63
00:03:02,374 --> 00:03:05,579
Which answer should the program convert
with float?

64
00:03:16,947 --> 00:03:17,063
A.

65
00:03:17,083 --> 00:03:18,090
The bill total.

66
00:03:18,110 --> 00:03:19,526
It can have decimals.

67
00:03:19,546 --> 00:03:22,879
The other two are whole numbers, so they
use int.

68
00:03:23,487 --> 00:03:24,477
Take a breather.

69
00:03:24,497 --> 00:03:27,887
You know the problem, the mission and what
done means.

70
00:03:27,933 --> 00:03:33,370
Next, Asha plans the tool with an AI
assistant, before she writes any code.

71
00:03:38,117 --> 00:03:39,200
Stage 1: plan.

72
00:03:39,220 --> 00:03:43,377
Asha tells the AI what she knows, and what
she wants.

73
00:03:43,397 --> 00:03:45,427
And she says: no code yet.

74
00:03:45,467 --> 00:03:50,580
The AI lists the inputs with their types,
the calculations in order, and the

75
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:51,121
outputs.

76
00:03:51,141 --> 00:03:52,337
It is a good plan.

77
00:03:52,367 --> 00:03:57,695
But look at the last calculation: each
share is the total, divided by the people.

78
00:03:57,715 --> 00:04:00,733
That is exactly what the phone calculator
did.

79
00:04:01,353 --> 00:04:03,750
Asha remembers the mission.

80
00:04:03,770 --> 00:04:08,673
43.07 divided by 4 is 10.7675, and nobody
can pay that.

81
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:14,182
So she adds one step to the plan: work in
whole cents.

82
00:04:14,202 --> 00:04:16,220
43.07 is 4307 cents.

83
00:04:16,267 --> 00:04:23,078
Whole numbers share out with double slash,
and the remainder operator gives what is

84
00:04:23,098 --> 00:04:23,901
left over.

85
00:04:23,921 --> 00:04:27,193
You did this with biscuits in Chapter 2.

86
00:04:27,787 --> 00:04:28,828
Stage 2: write.

87
00:04:28,848 --> 00:04:31,657
Asha writes the three questions herself.

88
00:04:31,677 --> 00:04:36,397
Then she asks the AI to check the types,
without changing anything.

89
00:04:36,433 --> 00:04:41,471
The AI agrees with her: a float for the
bill, and an int for the tip percent and

90
00:04:41,491 --> 00:04:42,420
for the people.

91
00:04:42,440 --> 00:04:44,316
You cannot have half a person!

92
00:04:44,917 --> 00:04:45,956
Time to build.

93
00:04:45,976 --> 00:04:51,027
Line 2 prints a centred header, with
equals signs, as in Chapter 3.

94
00:04:51,067 --> 00:04:53,945
Lines 3 to 5 ask the three questions.

95
00:04:53,965 --> 00:04:57,627
Each answer is converted as soon as it is
read.

96
00:04:57,667 --> 00:04:59,370
Line 6 is only a check.

97
00:04:59,390 --> 00:05:01,918
It prints the type of each answer.

98
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:13,554
Int.

99
00:05:13,574 --> 00:05:19,060
The answer was the text 3, and int on line
5 turned it into a whole number.

100
00:05:19,100 --> 00:05:21,806
The finished program does not need line 6.

101
00:05:21,826 --> 00:05:23,558
It was only there to check.

102
00:05:24,150 --> 00:05:25,526
Step 2: the tip.

103
00:05:25,546 --> 00:05:27,010
First, the maths.

104
00:05:27,030 --> 00:05:29,890
A percent is a number out of 100.

105
00:05:29,933 --> 00:05:34,894
So 10 percent of 48 is 48 times 10,
divided by 100.

106
00:05:34,914 --> 00:05:36,164
That is 4.80.

107
00:05:36,184 --> 00:05:40,558
Asha works this out by hand, before any
code.

108
00:05:41,153 --> 00:05:42,509
Stage 3: generate.

109
00:05:42,529 --> 00:05:47,933
Asha asks the AI for the tip and total
lines, and asks what they print.

110
00:05:47,967 --> 00:05:53,535
The AI writes a few short lines, and says
they print a tip of 4.80.

111
00:05:53,555 --> 00:05:54,787
It sounds sure.

112
00:05:54,833 --> 00:05:56,746
Read line 4 carefully.

113
00:06:08,113 --> 00:06:08,346
No.

114
00:06:08,366 --> 00:06:12,561
Line 4 multiplies by 10, and never divides
by 100.

115
00:06:12,581 --> 00:06:15,089
Let's see what really happens.

116
00:06:15,687 --> 00:06:16,781
Stage 4: test.

117
00:06:16,801 --> 00:06:18,373
Asha runs the lines.

118
00:06:18,393 --> 00:06:19,567
The tip is 480!

119
00:06:19,587 --> 00:06:22,512
Ten times bigger than the whole bill.

120
00:06:22,532 --> 00:06:25,617
Her hand result catches it immediately.

121
00:06:25,667 --> 00:06:28,596
She shows the AI the real output.

122
00:06:28,616 --> 00:06:34,227
This time it names the cause: a percent
must be divided by 100.

123
00:06:34,267 --> 00:06:35,669
She runs the fix.

124
00:06:35,689 --> 00:06:37,927
4.80, and a total of 52.80.

125
00:06:37,947 --> 00:06:41,189
Now the code and her hand result agree.

126
00:06:41,783 --> 00:06:44,410
Step 3, the most important part.

127
00:06:44,430 --> 00:06:49,703
Asha uses the mission's numbers: a total
of 43.07, and 4 people.

128
00:06:49,733 --> 00:06:52,330
Line 4 multiplies by 100 to get cents.

129
00:06:52,350 --> 00:06:57,701
It uses round, because a float can be a
tiny amount below the whole number you

130
00:06:57,721 --> 00:06:58,183
expect.

131
00:06:58,233 --> 00:07:03,273
Line 5 shares the cents with double slash:
whole cents only.

132
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:09,640
Line 6 uses the remainder operator to find
the cents left over.

133
00:07:09,667 --> 00:07:12,342
Lines 7 to 9 print the three numbers.

134
00:07:12,362 --> 00:07:17,733
And line 10 is a check: each share times
the people, plus the extra cents.

135
00:07:29,113 --> 00:07:31,786
4307, the total in cents.

136
00:07:31,806 --> 00:07:33,078
And it does!

137
00:07:33,098 --> 00:07:36,093
Every cent is accounted for.

138
00:07:36,133 --> 00:07:41,291
Each person pays 1076 cents, and 3 cents
are left over.

139
00:07:41,887 --> 00:07:42,732
Quick check.

140
00:07:42,752 --> 00:07:44,825
Seven people share 100 cents.

141
00:07:44,845 --> 00:07:47,350
How many extra cents are left over?

142
00:07:58,713 --> 00:07:59,182
C, 2.

143
00:07:59,202 --> 00:08:01,726
Each person gets 14 cents.

144
00:08:01,746 --> 00:08:05,541
14 times 7 is 98, and 2 cents are left.

145
00:08:06,117 --> 00:08:06,703
Step 4.

146
00:08:06,723 --> 00:08:10,599
To show money again, divide the cents by
100.

147
00:08:10,619 --> 00:08:12,677
1760 cents becomes 17.6.

148
00:08:12,700 --> 00:08:14,630
Line 4 prints 17.6.

149
00:08:14,650 --> 00:08:18,530
Python drops the last zero of a float.

150
00:08:18,567 --> 00:08:23,317
Line 5 adds dot 2 f, so it looks like
money: 17.60.

151
00:08:23,367 --> 00:08:30,471
And line 6 puts it in a receipt line: 16
characters for the label, 8 for the

152
00:08:30,491 --> 00:08:31,127
amount.

153
00:08:31,717 --> 00:08:33,379
Step 5: put it together.

154
00:08:33,399 --> 00:08:37,867
Lines 2 to 5 are the header and the three
questions from step 1.

155
00:08:37,900 --> 00:08:43,850
Lines 7 and 8 are the tip and the total,
with the percent divided by 100.

156
00:08:43,900 --> 00:08:48,120
Lines 9 to 12 are the whole cents from
steps 3 and 4.

157
00:08:48,167 --> 00:08:54,308
Line 14 builds the tip label with an f
string, so the receipt says Tip 10

158
00:08:54,328 --> 00:08:54,983
percent.

159
00:08:55,003 --> 00:08:57,937
Line 15 makes a line of 24 hyphens.

160
00:08:57,967 --> 00:09:00,390
Lines 16 to 22 print the receipt.

161
00:09:00,410 --> 00:09:06,239
The last line prints the extra cents
without dot 2 f, because cents are a whole

162
00:09:06,259 --> 00:09:06,758
number.

163
00:09:18,113 --> 00:09:18,794
17.60.

164
00:09:18,814 --> 00:09:26,733
5280 cents, shared by 3, is 1760 cents
each, with nothing left over.

165
00:09:26,767 --> 00:09:33,123
Every line matches the destination you saw
at the start.

166
00:09:33,720 --> 00:09:34,270
Breather.

167
00:09:34,290 --> 00:09:35,410
The tool is built.

168
00:09:35,430 --> 00:09:37,690
Roll your shoulders and breathe out.

169
00:09:37,733 --> 00:09:39,675
But built is not finished.

170
00:09:39,695 --> 00:09:41,787
Criterion six says: test it.

171
00:09:46,517 --> 00:09:51,367
A test is a set of answers whose correct
result you worked out first.

172
00:09:51,387 --> 00:09:52,497
Asha plans four.

173
00:09:52,533 --> 00:09:55,563
The normal case, which you ran in step 5.

174
00:09:55,600 --> 00:09:58,640
A bill with decimals: the case from the
mission.

175
00:09:58,667 --> 00:10:00,257
A tip of zero.

176
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:02,257
And one person, who pays it all.

177
00:10:02,853 --> 00:10:09,609
Test 2 runs the same program with the
mission's numbers: 37.45, a 15 percent

178
00:10:09,629 --> 00:10:11,213
tip, and 4 people.

179
00:10:11,233 --> 00:10:14,738
The total is 4307 cents, shared by 4.

180
00:10:14,758 --> 00:10:19,025
You already worked these cents out in step
3.

181
00:10:30,380 --> 00:10:31,939
Let's run it and see.

182
00:10:32,553 --> 00:10:37,181
Each pays 10.76, with 3 extra cents.

183
00:10:37,201 --> 00:10:45,443
Four times 10.76 is 43.04, and 3 cents
more makes exactly 43.07.

184
00:10:45,467 --> 00:10:50,713
The tip, 5.6175, shows as 5.62.

185
00:10:51,320 --> 00:10:52,961
Test 3, no tip.

186
00:10:52,981 --> 00:10:59,492
The tip is 0.00, and each person pays
10.00, as worked out.

187
00:11:00,087 --> 00:11:01,745
Test 4, one person.

188
00:11:01,765 --> 00:11:06,513
The total is 13.75, and the one person
pays all of it.

189
00:11:06,533 --> 00:11:08,280
All four tests pass.

190
00:11:08,887 --> 00:11:09,875
Stage 5: debug.

191
00:11:09,895 --> 00:11:14,649
A friend tries to break the tool, and
types 0 for the number of people.

192
00:11:14,669 --> 00:11:16,800
The program stops with an error.

193
00:11:16,820 --> 00:11:18,347
Asha reads it: line 10.

194
00:11:18,367 --> 00:11:23,027
Asha pastes the lines and the full error,
and asks for the cause first.

195
00:11:23,047 --> 00:11:26,917
The AI explains: nothing can be shared
between zero people.

196
00:11:26,967 --> 00:11:30,667
To refuse a bad answer, the program must
make a decision.

197
00:11:30,687 --> 00:11:33,473
That needs if, which you meet in Chapter
6.

198
00:11:33,493 --> 00:11:35,562
For now, a clear question helps.

199
00:11:36,153 --> 00:11:36,718
Breather.

200
00:11:36,738 --> 00:11:39,703
Four tests passed, and one test found a
limit.

201
00:11:39,733 --> 00:11:43,268
Last stage: can the AI make the tool
better?

202
00:11:48,017 --> 00:11:49,221
Stage 6: improve.

203
00:11:49,241 --> 00:11:55,197
Asha asks for one way to make the program
simpler, without changing what it prints.

204
00:11:55,233 --> 00:12:01,440
The AI suggests replacing the two share
lines, 10 and 12, with one line: round the

205
00:12:01,460 --> 00:12:04,553
total divided by the people, to 2 places.

206
00:12:04,600 --> 00:12:05,449
Shorter, yes.

207
00:12:05,469 --> 00:12:08,257
Think about the mission before you answer.

208
00:12:19,613 --> 00:12:20,893
Asha does not guess.

209
00:12:20,913 --> 00:12:23,493
She tests it with the mission's numbers.

210
00:12:24,087 --> 00:12:26,152
Line 4 is the AI's line.

211
00:12:26,172 --> 00:12:28,847
Line 6 adds up the four shares.

212
00:12:28,867 --> 00:12:32,451
Each pays 10.77, and the shares add up to
43.08.

213
00:12:32,471 --> 00:12:36,507
One cent too much: the exact problem from
the mission!

214
00:12:36,533 --> 00:12:39,124
So Asha keeps her cent lines.

215
00:12:39,144 --> 00:12:44,615
A suggestion that changes the result is
not a simplification.

216
00:12:45,220 --> 00:12:47,920
Here are all six stages on one tool.

217
00:12:47,967 --> 00:12:49,927
The AI helped every time.

218
00:12:49,947 --> 00:12:54,047
It planned, checked, wrote, explained and
suggested.

219
00:12:54,067 --> 00:12:57,427
And it was wrong twice: the tip, and the
rounding.

220
00:12:57,467 --> 00:12:59,444
Both times, a test caught it.

221
00:12:59,464 --> 00:13:01,855
The same test you work out by hand.

222
00:13:02,453 --> 00:13:03,317
Quick check.

223
00:13:03,337 --> 00:13:05,823
Your program passes all its tests.

224
00:13:05,843 --> 00:13:08,033
An AI suggests a simpler line.

225
00:13:08,053 --> 00:13:09,580
What do you do first?

226
00:13:20,947 --> 00:13:21,103
D.

227
00:13:21,123 --> 00:13:24,538
Run your tests again with the new line.

228
00:13:24,558 --> 00:13:27,533
That is how Asha caught the 43.08.

229
00:13:28,153 --> 00:13:29,129
Last breather.

230
00:13:29,149 --> 00:13:34,532
You built, tested and improved a real
tool, with an AI as a partner, and you

231
00:13:34,552 --> 00:13:35,883
made the decisions.

232
00:13:35,933 --> 00:13:36,970
Let's recap.

233
00:13:41,717 --> 00:13:44,057
Here is what you used in this boss level.

234
00:13:44,100 --> 00:13:48,420
Plan first: inputs, calculations in order,
then outputs.

235
00:13:48,467 --> 00:13:50,765
Convert each answer as you read it.

236
00:13:50,785 --> 00:13:53,017
Float for amounts, int for counts.

237
00:13:53,067 --> 00:13:55,837
A percent is divided by 100.

238
00:13:55,867 --> 00:14:00,976
Share money in whole cents: double slash
for each share, and the remainder operator

239
00:14:00,996 --> 00:14:02,027
for what is left.

240
00:14:02,067 --> 00:14:07,087
And test with results you worked out
first, including every AI suggestion.

241
00:14:07,687 --> 00:14:08,815
One last check.

242
00:14:08,835 --> 00:14:12,718
Which line calculates a 15 percent tip on
the bill?

243
00:14:24,080 --> 00:14:24,272
B.

244
00:14:24,292 --> 00:14:26,920
Times 15, divided by 100.

245
00:14:26,940 --> 00:14:29,991
Exactly the fix from stage 4.

246
00:14:30,587 --> 00:14:32,150
Boss level complete!

247
00:14:32,170 --> 00:14:35,553
You earned 300 XP and the Fair Share
badge.

248
00:14:35,573 --> 00:14:37,215
And Module 1 is done.

249
00:14:39,320 --> 00:14:44,270
The practice file has eight more money
tasks, worth 170 XP.

250
00:14:44,300 --> 00:14:48,560
Three warm-ups, worth 10 XP each,
including sharing sweets.

251
00:14:48,600 --> 00:14:53,060
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each, such as
a fixed service charge.

252
00:14:53,100 --> 00:14:55,750
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

253
